The one-spin partition function is $\begin{aligned}
z_{\rm mf}
&=\sum_{\sigma=\pm1}
\exp\!\left[-\beta(-2dJm+h)\sigma\right] \\
&=2\cosh\!\left[\beta(2dJm-h)\right].\end{aligned}$ Its average spin is $\begin{aligned}
\expect{\sigma}
&=\frac{\sum_{\sigma=\pm1}\sigma
 e^{\beta(2dJm-h)\sigma}}
{\sum_{\sigma=\pm1}e^{\beta(2dJm-h)\sigma}} \\
&=\tanh\!\left[\beta(2dJm-h)\right].\end{aligned}$ But the spin we selected is supposed to be typical of the neighbors whose average we called $m$. Self-consistency therefore requires $\boxed{m=\tanh\!\left[\beta(2dJm-h)\right].}$ This one nonlinear equation is the mean-field reduction of the many-spin problem.

